{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Southeast Asia’s digital economy to hit $368 billion\nSource: GOOGLE AI BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\n1. AI is sustaining growth in every sector.\n2. Consumer trust will help unlock the era of agentic commerce.\n3. Four major areas must adapt to enable AI adoption.\nGoogle AI Blog\nSoutheast Asia’s digital economy to hit $368 billion\nOctober 21, 2026\n4 min read\n\nIn Southeast Asia, generative AI has become an essential part of daily life, actively used by 243 million people (58% of the working population) and scaling four times faster than the rollout of the mobile internet. This has major implications for the region’s economy, according to the 11th edition of the annual e-Conomy SEA report 1 . Building the Intelligent Economy, by Google, Temasek, and Bain & Company, finds that widespread AI implementation is propelling the region’s digital economy to grow 17% year-on-year (YoY) to reach $368 billion 2 in Gross Merchandise Value (GMV) in 2026. This puts it firmly on track to exceed $1 trillion by 2035. Using tokens 3 as the new benchmark for the AI economy, the report projects that growing confidence in agentic commerce will drive a 60-fold surge in regional AI usage over the next five years.\n\nHere are the three key takeaways from this year's e-Conomy SEA 2026 report:\n\n1. AI is sustaining growth in every sector.\n\nAcross the digital economy, AI is reshaping how people live, shop, and move. E-commerce ($226B GMV, +18% YoY) anchors this shift, as shoppers use multimodal discovery and in-app assistants to confidently navigate complex purchases. These personalized experiences organically drive 10% higher conversions and order volume uplifts as large as 75%.\n\nIn online travel ($59B GMV, +10% YoY), traditional search engines remain the top platform people use to plan travel, while generative AI has overtaken social media to become the second-most used tool for organizing complex itineraries.\n\nOnline media ($44B revenue, +22% YoY) is personalizing content feeds and connecting audiences with more relevant ads, yielding a 10 to 20% quality engagement uplift as commerce media becomes a $4 billion market.\n\nMobility and food delivery ($40B GMV, +15% YoY) saves consumers time via predictive suggestions, smarter routing, and AI-powered offerings like pooled rides, fueling healthy transactional growth.\n\nFinally, digital financial services ($1,650B GTV, +16% YoY) help consumers with real-time fraud detection, broader credit access, and personalized financial recommendations that complement human advice, alongside Singapore’s regional leadership in digital wealth platforms.\n\n2. Consumer trust will help unlock the era of agentic commerce.\n\nDigital automation is already well established across the region, where 72% of users rely on everyday features like price tracking. This familiarity is clearing the runway for agentic commerce, with 75% expressing willingness to let AI agents autonomously compare prices and complete transactions on their behalf.\n\nThis momentum is rooted in regional optimism: people in Southeast Asia view AI as a net benefit to society, with 62% reporting greater trust in AI tools today than a year ago. Driven by proven track records (56%), rising familiarity (55%), and output accuracy (53%), this trust signals a clear runway for deeper platform integration. To feel comfortable using new agentic capabilities, users expect clarity and guardrails, including robust privacy safeguards (44%), transparent information on what AI can and cannot do (36%), and the ability to reverse agentic transactions (30%).\n\n3. Four major areas must adapt to enable AI adoption.\n\nTo support a projected 60-fold increase in regional AI usage by 2030, the region's broader digital ecosystem must systematically evolve across four critical conditions:\n\nAccelerated agentic adoption and sustainable economics: Businesses need to move beyond using AI to answer single questions and start using it to help complete multi-step tasks from start to finish, backed by strong privacy safeguards and clear safety rules. At the same time, running AI models must  …[truncated]","response":"In this article\n\n1. AI is sustaining growth in every sector.\n2. Consumer trust will help unlock the era of agentic commerce.\n3. Four major areas must adapt to enable AI adoption.\nGoogle AI Blog\nSoutheast Asia’s digital economy to hit $368 billion\nOctober 21, 2026\n4 min read\n\nIn Southeast Asia, generative AI has become an essential part of daily life, actively used by 243 million people (58% of the working population) and scaling four times faster than the rollout of the mobile internet. This has major implications for the region’s economy, according to the 11th edition of the annual e-Conomy SEA report 1 . Building the Intelligent Economy, by Google, Temasek, and Bain & Company, finds that widespread AI implementation is propelling the region’s digital economy to grow 17% year-on-year (YoY) to reach $368 billion 2 in Gross Merchandise Value (GMV) in 2026. This puts it firmly on track to exceed $1 trillion by 2035. Using tokens 3 as the new benchmark for the AI economy, the report projects that growing confidence in agentic commerce will drive a 60-fold surge in regional AI usage over the next five years.\n\nHere are the three key takeaways from this year's e-Conomy SEA 2026 report:\n\n1. AI is sustaining growth in every sector.\n\nAcross the digital economy, AI is reshaping how people live, shop, and move. E-commerce ($226B GMV, +18% YoY) anchors this shift, as shoppers use multimodal discovery and in-app assistants to confidently navigate complex purchases. These personalized experiences organically drive 10% higher conversions and order volume uplifts as large as 75%.\n\nIn online travel ($59B GMV, +10% YoY), traditional search engines remain the top platform people use to plan travel, while generative AI has overtaken social media to become the second-most used tool for organizing complex itineraries.\n\nOnline media ($44B revenue, +22% YoY) is personalizing content feeds and connecting audiences with more relevant ads, yielding a 10 to 20% quality engagement uplift as commerce media becomes a $4 billion market.\n\nMobility and food delivery ($40B GMV, +15% YoY) saves consumers time via predictive suggestions, smarter routing, and AI-powered offerings like pooled rides, fueling healthy transactional growth.\n\nFinally, digital financial services ($1,650B GTV, +16% YoY) help consumers with real-time fraud detection, broader credit access, and personalized financial recommendations that complement human advice, alongside Singapore’s regional leadership in digital wealth platforms.\n\n2. Consumer trust will help unlock the era of agentic commerce.\n\nDigital automation is already well established across the region, where 72% of users rely on everyday features like price tracking. This familiarity is clearing the runway for agentic commerce, with 75% expressing willingness to let AI agents autonomously compare prices and complete transactions on their behalf.\n\nThis momentum is rooted in regional optimism: people in Southeast Asia view AI as a net benefit to society, with 62% reporting greater trust in AI tools today than a year ago. Driven by proven track records (56%), rising familiarity (55%), and output accuracy (53%), this trust signals a clear runway for deeper platform integration. To feel comfortable using new agentic capabilities, users expect clarity and guardrails, including robust privacy safeguards (44%), transparent information on what AI can and cannot do (36%), and the ability to reverse agentic transactions (30%).\n\n3. Four major areas must adapt to enable AI adoption.\n\nTo support a projected 60-fold increase in regional AI usage by 2030, the region's broader digital ecosystem must systematically evolve across four critical conditions:\n\nAccelerated agentic adoption and sustainable economics: Businesses need to move beyond using AI to answer single questions and start using it to help complete multi-step tasks from start to finish, backed by strong privacy safeguards and clear safety rules. At the same time, running AI models must  …[truncated]","reasoning":"The modal contains a full, substantive article with multiple coherent paragraphs covering a clear topic (Southeast Asia's digital economy and AI adoption), including detailed statistics, sector breakdowns, and key takeaways.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:34:41.488Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nSource: TESTOMAT BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\nWhat Is Integration Testing in Software Testing?\nContract Test vs Integration Test: Which Failures Do They Catch?\nWhen to Use Contract Testing and Integration Testing in the Test Pyramid\nAPI Testing Tools for Contract Testing and Integration Testing\nHow Testomat.io Fits Contract Testing and Integration Testing for QA Teams\nCommon Mistakes When You Implement Contract Testing\nBottom Line\nTestomat Blog\nContract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nTetiana Khomenko\nOctober 21, 2026\n12 min read\n\nYour orders service calls the payment service. The payment team renames a response field and merges. Every unit test stays green and the integration suite reports the failure later. A study in the Journal of Systems and Software names poor communication of API changes as a core challenge of microservice teams. This contract testing vs integration testing guide compares both testing approaches for API and microservices teams and shows where both results belong. Keep on reading to discover key differences between contract testing and integration testing.\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\n\nContract testing checks that a consumer and a provider agree on the request and response format, with each side tested alone. Integration testing runs multiple components together with real dependencies. The two test types answer different questions:\n\nA contract test asks whether the payment service sends the fields that the orders service reads.\nAn integration test asks whether the orders service and the payment service work together with the database in a real flow.\n\nYou need both answers before a release, and the table shows how contract testing compares to integration testing.\n\nAspect\tContract testing\tIntegration testing\nScope\tThe agreement of a consumer and a provider\tSeveral components in a real flow\nWhat runs\tEach side alone, with a mock of the other side\tAll components together\nDependencies\tA contract file replaces the other service\tReal database and real services\nSpeed\tSeconds, like a unit suite\tMinutes, with a running environment\nWho writes it\tThe consumer team and the provider team\tDevelopers and QA engineers\nA failure shows\tA changed field or a changed status code\tWrong data or a timeout\nEnvironment\tA single service and a mock\tA full test environment\nCI stage\tEvery pull request of both services\tAfter the merge and on a schedule\n\nThe contract test vs integration test question has a short answer: a contract test protects the API agreement, and an integration test protects the real behavior in a test environment that mirrors production. The next sections explain each way of testing and show which failures each type catches.\n\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\n\nContract testing is a testing approach that checks that a service consumer and a service provider agree on their API. The contract is a predefined file with the expected request and response pairs. The consumer is the API client that sends requests, for example the orders service. The provider is the service that answers, for example the payment service. The Pact documentation describes contract testing as “testing an integration point by checking each application in isolation”.\n\nEach side works in isolation and reads the same contract file, so independent services can deploy at different times and development teams avoid compatibility issues at release time. Automated contract testing works well for REST APIs, and contract testing can also cover message queues. The contract covers only the fields the consumer reads. The provider can add new fields, and the contract test stays green. The provider renames or removes a field the consumer reads, and the contract test fails in the pull request of the provider. This early detection prevents integration failures that an end-to-end test would report days later.\n\nConsumer-Driven Contract Testing Wi …[truncated]","response":"In this article\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\nWhat Is Integration Testing in Software Testing?\nContract Test vs Integration Test: Which Failures Do They Catch?\nWhen to Use Contract Testing and Integration Testing in the Test Pyramid\nAPI Testing Tools for Contract Testing and Integration Testing\nHow Testomat.io Fits Contract Testing and Integration Testing for QA Teams\nCommon Mistakes When You Implement Contract Testing\nBottom Line\nTestomat Blog\nContract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nTetiana Khomenko\nOctober 21, 2026\n12 min read\n\nYour orders service calls the payment service. The payment team renames a response field and merges. Every unit test stays green and the integration suite reports the failure later. A study in the Journal of Systems and Software names poor communication of API changes as a core challenge of microservice teams. This contract testing vs integration testing guide compares both testing approaches for API and microservices teams and shows where both results belong. Keep on reading to discover key differences between contract testing and integration testing.\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\n\nContract testing checks that a consumer and a provider agree on the request and response format, with each side tested alone. Integration testing runs multiple components together with real dependencies. The two test types answer different questions:\n\nA contract test asks whether the payment service sends the fields that the orders service reads.\nAn integration test asks whether the orders service and the payment service work together with the database in a real flow.\n\nYou need both answers before a release, and the table shows how contract testing compares to integration testing.\n\nAspect\tContract testing\tIntegration testing\nScope\tThe agreement of a consumer and a provider\tSeveral components in a real flow\nWhat runs\tEach side alone, with a mock of the other side\tAll components together\nDependencies\tA contract file replaces the other service\tReal database and real services\nSpeed\tSeconds, like a unit suite\tMinutes, with a running environment\nWho writes it\tThe consumer team and the provider team\tDevelopers and QA engineers\nA failure shows\tA changed field or a changed status code\tWrong data or a timeout\nEnvironment\tA single service and a mock\tA full test environment\nCI stage\tEvery pull request of both services\tAfter the merge and on a schedule\n\nThe contract test vs integration test question has a short answer: a contract test protects the API agreement, and an integration test protects the real behavior in a test environment that mirrors production. The next sections explain each way of testing and show which failures each type catches.\n\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\n\nContract testing is a testing approach that checks that a service consumer and a service provider agree on their API. The contract is a predefined file with the expected request and response pairs. The consumer is the API client that sends requests, for example the orders service. The provider is the service that answers, for example the payment service. The Pact documentation describes contract testing as “testing an integration point by checking each application in isolation”.\n\nEach side works in isolation and reads the same contract file, so independent services can deploy at different times and development teams avoid compatibility issues at release time. Automated contract testing works well for REST APIs, and contract testing can also cover message queues. The contract covers only the fields the consumer reads. The provider can add new fields, and the contract test stays green. The provider renames or removes a field the consumer reads, and the contract test fails in the pull request of the provider. This early detection prevents integration failures that an end-to-end test would report days later.\n\nConsumer-Driven Contract Testing Wi …[truncated]","reasoning":"The text is a full article with multiple sections, definitions, examples, and a comparison table, providing substantial information on contract testing vs integration testing. It is clearly a legitimate article body, though truncated at the end due to modal limits.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:34:52.055Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: How to Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack | mabl\nSource: MABL BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nKey Takeaways\nWhy Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack?\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Layers at a Glance\nLayer 1: Models and Reasoning\nLayer 2: Orchestration, Planning, and Agent Workflows\nLayer 3: Tools, MCP, and Action Interfaces\nLayer 4: Memory, Context, and Retrieval\nLayer 5: Guardrails, Permissions, and Governance\nLayer 6: Observability and AI Agent Evaluation\nLayer 7: Testing and Independent Verification\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Checklist Before Production\nHow mabl Fits Into the Agentic Development Tech Stack\nMabl Blog\nHow to Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack\nAbbey Charles\nOctober 20, 2026\n8 min read\nKey Takeaways\nAn agentic development tech stack combines models, orchestration, tools, memory, guardrails, observability, evaluations, and testing for production use.\nAI and code models power agent reasoning and code generation. The surrounding stack provides context, tools, permissions, workflow controls, and independent verification.\nTesting should sit beside evaluations and observability to verify generated code across real product, user, and system workflows.\nStrong stacks give agents clear tasks and controlled access while people retain authority over risk, governance, and release decisions.\n\n\nWhat is an agentic development tech stack? It's the set of systems that help AI agents complete software work safely and reliably.\n\nThe stack can include AI models, orchestration, tools, memory, guardrails, observability, evaluations, and testing.\n\nTogether, these layers help AI agents plan work, modify code, use development tools, and respond to feedback.\n\nThey also give your team control over access, risk, approvals, verification, and release decisions. Those controls become more important as agentic development changes how code is created, reviewed, tested, and released.\n\nModels and prompts may start the process. Production use depends on the systems surrounding them.\n\nAn agentic testing platform brings independent verification into the stack from the beginning.\n\nWhy Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack?\n\nCoding assistants can suggest functions, explain errors, or generate small changes.\n\nAgentic workflows handle longer sequences of work throughout the delivery process.\n\nAn agent may read a ticket, inspect several files, call tools, run checks, and prepare a pull request. Each step depends on reliable context, controlled access, and clear feedback.\n\nWithout a defined stack, those responsibilities often become scattered across separate tools and team-owned integrations:\n\nPermissions may vary\nContext can get lost\nActions become harder to trace\nTesting may arrive too late\n\nAn agentic development tech stack brings those systems into one operating model. It connects the tools agents use with the controls, evidence, and quality checks your team needs.\n\nOnce agents can modify code or trigger delivery systems, teams need clear approval paths and reliable verification. They also need a record of what changed, what the agent did, and how the outcome was validated.\n\nA well-designed stack gives you consistent control across the workflow. You can limit higher-risk actions, route decisions for approval, and verify outcomes before release.\n\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Layers at a Glance\n\nThe layers below show the systems needed to support agentic development from initial reasoning through final verification.\n\nStack Layer\n\n\t\n\nWhat It Does\n\n\t\n\nCommon Examples\n\n\n\n\nModels\n\n\t\n\nProvide reasoning, generation, and code assistance.\n\n\t\n\nLLMs, code models, 